Prime Video has sparked speculation among Panchayat fans about the future of the popular series following a cryptic new message shared by the streaming platform. The latest development has left viewers wondering whether the show will return with a fifth season or if they will have to wait longer to revisit the village of Phulera.The speculation comes after the events of Season 4, which brought a major change in Phulera's political landscape. Pradhan Ji (Raghubir Yadav) and Manju Devi (Neena Gupta) lost their positions, paving the way for new leadership in the village. Kranti Devi (Sunita Rajwar) took over as the new Pradhan, with her husband, Bhushan Sharma, popularly known as Banrakas (Durgesh Kumar), supporting her.&nbsp;
View this post on Instagram
&nbsp;
A post shared by prime video IN (@primevideoin)
With this change in leadership, the dynamics in Phulera have also shifted. Banrakas now appears to have a greater say in the village's affairs, raising questions about how the new power structure will shape the story going forward.However, Prime Video's latest cryptic message has added another layer of uncertainty surrounding Season 5. While the post has prompted fans to speculate about the show's return, the streaming platform has not clarified what lies ahead for the series.Does this mean Panchayat will not return this year, or is the message simply teasing a new development in Phulera? With Banrakas and Kranti Devi now at the helm, viewers are left wondering whether the village's story is heading towards an unexpected new chapter or if they will have to wait longer for the next season.Also Read : Pankaj Tripathi, Manoj Bajpayee, Karan Tacker, Bobby Deol and Jitendra Kumar: The OTT stars whose shows keep coming back

The 57th International Film Festival of India (IFFI) will bring together filmmakers, cinema enthusiasts and creative voices from across the world in Goa from 20 to 28 November 2026, under the theme ‘Stories Without Borders’. Reflecting its growing global reach, the festival has received over 1,900 film submissions from 137 countries and will showcase more than 150 international films representing 86 countries, creating a vibrant platform for cross-cultural exchange, diverse storytelling and cinematic excellence.The Curtain Raiser Press Conference of the 57th International Film Festival of India (IFFI) 2026, held at Taj Cidade de Goa Horizon, Goa, on November 13, was inaugurated by the Chief Minister of Goa, Dr Pramod Sawant, who unveiled the festival’s expansive programme celebrating Indian and international cinema. Expressing pride in Goa’s association with IFFI, he described the festival as an integral part of the state’s cultural identity and calendar. He also highlighted the state government’s preparations to welcome the global film fraternity, with arrangements for screening venues, transport, hospitality and public facilities.The Curtain Raiser Press event was also attended by M Delilah M. Lobo, Vice Chairperson, Entertainment Society of Goa, Chanchal Kumar, Secretary, Ministry of Information and Broadcasting, Government of India, Krishna Kumar, Chairperson, NFDC, and Ashutosh Gowariker, Festival Director, IFFI 2026, Prabhat, Additional Secretary, Ministry of Information and Broadcasting; Madhur Bhandarkar, Chairperson, Feature Film Jury; Himanshu Shekhar Khatua, Chairperson, Non-Feature Film Jury; Deepak Mandekar, CEO, Entertainment Society of Goa; and Dheeraj Singh, Managing Director, NFDC, &amp; Smita Vats Sharma, DG West Zone PIB.The opening film, Fatherland, directed by Paweł Pawlikowski, follows Nobel laureate Thomas Mann and his daughter Erika as they travel through post-war, divided Germany in 1949, exploring the political and personal divisions of the time. The film received the Best Director Award at the 2026 Cannes Film Festival and was also nominated for the Sydney Film Prize 2026.The 57th International Film Festival of India (IFFI) 2026 will showcase I Matter, directed by Alina Șerban, as its Mid-Fest Film. A 101-minute film co-produced by Romania, Germany and Belgium, it features dialogues in Romanian and Romani, bringing a diverse European cinematic perspective to the festival.&nbsp;
