&nbsp;
A post shared by Netflix India (@netflix_in)
Tanya Bami, Series Head, Netflix India, also spoke about the upcoming show and the platform's plans for the romance genre. She said, “Romance is such a loved genre and yet so under served in the series format. After Musafir Cafe, we are extremely excited to bring our next offering in the romance genre - Imtiaz Ali's O Saathi Re. The complexity of this romance is what sets it apart as it takes something as familiar as love and sees it through the lens of a dynamic that always exists in a love story, raising a never asked question - is all love truly ever equal?Imtiaz has always had a wonderful understanding of people and the emotions that drive them and this love story through his lens of finding the deeper meaning in the everyday is something we can't wait for the audience to discover. After our wonderful partnership with him for Amar Singh Chamkila, there is something very special about seeing him bring his distinct storytelling sensibility to his next long-form series. With an incredible cast comprising Aditi Ra

Actor Nitish Bharadwaj has expressed disappointment with Ramayana Part 1 – The Rise of the Legend following its release, withdrawing his earlier appreciation of the film and raising concerns about its interpretation of the Hindu epic. Taking to Instagram, the actor shared a strongly worded note questioning the film’s portrayal of its central characters, particularly its depiction of Ravana played by Yash, and argued that visual spectacle cannot replace the philosophical and spiritual essence of the Ramayana.In his post, Nitish emphasised that the Ramayana is deeply embedded in India's civilizational and spiritual consciousness and that its characters cannot be redefined casually. Expressing his reservations about the film's interpretation, he wrote, “By presenting Ravan as an exceptionally liberal, socially just and fair figure, the film, in my view, alters the moral framework of the Ramayan and undermines the very purpose of avataar.”The actor further explained that the purpose of Lord Ram's incarnation was to uphold Dharma, tradition and Maryada. He maintained that the film's representation of the epic should remain mindful of the values and philosophical significance associated with its characters.One of the most significant aspects of his latest post was his decision to withdraw his earlier appreciation of the project. Expressing his disappointment in no uncertain terms, Nitish wrote, “I am deeply upset and disappointed with this film. I withdraw my earlier post about it. I cannot find a single good word to say about it.” He also criticised the film's reliance on visual effects, adding, “A spectacle of VFX can never substitute for the core values, philosophical depth and spiritual essence of this Mahakavya.”Nitish also questioned whether the film deserved to represent Indian cinema on the international stage, stating that, in his opinion, its narrative misrepresents India's history, heritage and core spiritual values. He concluded by emphasising the need for responsible representation of the country's cultural legacy.&nbsp;
View this post on Instagram
&nbsp;
